---
// DropShelf's privacy policy: the Privacy Policy URL for the App Store listing. Same layout as Emergency Tower's.
// It describes only what the app does (text from the DropShelf session). Keep it in step with the app if that changes.
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const EMAIL = 'njvanderbyl@gmail.com';
---

<Base
  title="Privacy policy · DropShelf"
  description="DropShelf collects no data: no account, no analytics, no advertising and no servers of its own. Everything on your shelves stays on your Mac."
  image="/og/dropshelf.jpg"
  imageAlt="DropShelf: your files, all in one place."
>
  <article class="doc shell">
    <p class="doc__crumb t-annotation"><a href={`${base}dropshelf/`}>DropShelf</a> · Privacy</p>
    <h1 class="doc__title">Privacy policy</h1>
    <p class="doc__meta t-annotation">Effective 25 November 2026 · Applies to the DropShelf app for Mac</p>

    <div class="doc__panel">
      <p>
        <strong>The short version.</strong> DropShelf doesn't collect any data. It has no account, no analytics, no advertising and no servers
        of its own. Everything you put on a shelf stays on your Mac. The only network connection it makes is
        through Apple's StoreKit, for the optional Pro purchase.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Who we are</h2>
    <p>
      DropShelf is made by Nathan VanderByl. Contact: <a
      href={`mailto:${EMAIL}?subject=DropShelf%20privacy`}>{EMAIL}</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Data we collect</h2>
    <p>
      None. DropShelf doesn't collect, transmit, sell or share personal data. There is no sign-in, no
      tracking, no analytics or crash-reporting service, and no advertising.
    </p>

    <h2>3. What stays on your Mac</h2>
    <p>
      DropShelf runs in Apple's App Sandbox and only sees files and folders you give it, by dropping them or
      choosing them. It stores the following on your Mac, inside its own app container:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Bookmarks to the items you add, so it can reopen them later. A file's path is used only as a display hint.</li>
      <li>Shelf names, item names, order, favourites and recent items.</li>
      <li>Independent copies of files you Copy or Move into DropShelf.</li>
      <li>Thumbnails, which can be regenerated.</li>
      <li>Transfer records used to recover safely from an interrupted Copy or Move.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      None of this is sent anywhere by DropShelf.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Linked items</h2>
    <p>
      For items you Link, DropShelf keeps a bookmark and never changes, renames or deletes the original.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Private shelves</h2>
    <p>
      Private shelves are hidden inside DropShelf until you authenticate with Touch ID or your Mac password,
      using Apple's LocalAuthentication. DropShelf never sees your fingerprint or password. Private shelves
      aren't encrypted: the files and DropShelf's own records of them are stored like any other, and macOS may
      index linked originals in Spotlight independently.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Spotlight and Siri</h2>
    <p>
      To make items searchable, DropShelf adds the names of items and shelves that aren't private to the
      Spotlight index on your Mac. Shortcuts and Siri use the same on-device information. Items on a private
      shelf are never added. You can turn this off in Settings > Privacy, which removes DropShelf's entries.
    </p>

    <h2>7. Purchases</h2>
    <p>
      DropShelf Pro is bought through Apple's App Store using StoreKit. Apple handles payment; DropShelf never
      sees your payment details. DropShelf checks Apple's signed purchase record to unlock Pro. Restore
      Purchases asks Apple for your existing purchase so Pro unlocks on another Mac signed in to the same
      Apple Account. It doesn't sync shelves or files.
    </p>

    <h2>8. Children</h2>
    <p>
      DropShelf doesn't collect data from anyone, including children.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Deleting your data</h2>
    <p>
      Deleting DropShelf and its container removes its library, including copies of files stored in DropShelf.
      Linked originals aren't affected. Export anything you want to keep first.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Changes</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the new version will be posted here with a new effective date.
    </p>

    <p class="doc__links t-annotation"><a href={`${base}dropshelf/`}>DropShelf</a> · <a href={`${base}dropshelf/support/`}>Support</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  :global(html) {
    scroll-snap-type: none !important;
  }

  :global(.site-header) {
    position: static !important;
  }

  .doc {
    --doc-accent: var(--color-dropshelf);
    padding-block: 56px 112px;
    max-width: calc(820px + 2 * var(--pad));
  }

  .doc__crumb {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 18px;
  }

  .doc__crumb a {
    text-decoration: none;
  }

  .doc__crumb a:hover {
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  .doc__title {
    font-size: clamp(2.6rem, 8vw, 5rem);
    line-height: 0.95;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
    margin-bottom: 14px;
  }

  .doc__lede,
  .doc__meta {
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 32px;
  }

  .doc__panel {
    padding: 20px 22px;
    margin-bottom: 40px;
    border: var(--border-hairline) solid var(--color-border);
    border-left: 4px solid var(--doc-accent);
    border-radius: 14px;
    background:
      radial-gradient(80% 120% at 100% 0%, color-mix(in srgb, var(--doc-accent) 12%, transparent), transparent 70%),
      var(--color-surface);
  }

  .doc__panel p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  .doc__links {
    margin-top: 48px;
    color: var(--color-muted);
  }

  h2 {
    font-size: 24px;
    line-height: 1.15;
    font-weight: 800;
    letter-spacing: -0.02em;
    margin: 36px 0 10px;
  }

  article ul {
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 0 0 14px;
    padding-left: 1.2em;
  }

  article p {
    max-width: 66ch;
    margin: 0 0 14px;
  }

  .doc__panel p {
    margin: 0;
  }
</style>
